{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"offline-outbox-storage-failure-fail-stop-follow-up","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Offline outbox storage failure: fail-stop follow-up"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Executable revision: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3d00a67fbc9509cde632578a9e36241caceb4e2"},{"type":"text","value":"\nPrior review: "},{"type":"link","href":"2026-09-30-offline-deletion-settlement.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Offline outbox deletion settlement"}]},{"type":"text","value":".\nReview inputs: the maintainer's four findings and test-gap note for PR #1968, plus CodeRabbit review 5373142655 at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"400d8d86970b2cc6368b30a68554bbdcad734bfd"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The maintainer decided that an outbox deletion failure means storage is seriously broken: stop the current executor and throw the storage error. This deliberately revises the earlier in-process deletion-retry law recorded at the prior executable revision. A phase-write failure takes the same fail-stop path because it prevents a durable record of the provider outcome."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"contract-and-evidence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Contract and evidence"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Boundary"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Executable witness at this revision"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Successful provider, failed delete"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"transaction-settlement.property.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" holds a provider and queues a peer. After delete fails, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commit()"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPersisted.promise"},{"type":"text","value":" reject with the exact storage error, the local transaction fails, the peer stays pending, and the head's durable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deletion-pending"},{"type":"text","value":" marker remains. An online notification causes no second deletion or peer provider call. A later admission rejects before its outbox write."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Permanent provider failure, failed delete"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The direct executor witness rejects "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute()"},{"type":"text","value":" with the exact storage error, rejects the transaction waiter with the original "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NonRetriableError"},{"type":"text","value":", and retains both FIFO entries. Another "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"executeAll()"},{"type":"text","value":" on the stopped executor rejects with the same storage error even after storage recovers."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Failed phase write"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The public witness rejects the head caller with the marker-write error, does not delete or retry, and leaves the queued peer untouched. The durable row is unmarked; a crash can still replay provider work."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Restart after a durable marker"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Separate successful and permanently rejected provider histories construct a fresh executor over the same storage. It removes the marked row without another provider call; the rejection path does not restore optimistic state. The successful path also checks that restart does not rewrite an already durable marker."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Timer and leadership boundary"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The leadership witness injects one failed terminal deletion, advances fake time by 60 seconds, and observes no automatic retry or later provider call."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The two direct stop-and-throw witnesses were RED against the uncommitted retrying candidate: both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute()"},{"type":"text","value":" calls resolved when they had to reject. They are GREEN at this revision. The full offline suite passed "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"237/237 tests across 18 files"}]},{"type":"text","value":"; package typecheck, build, Prettier, and edited-file lint passed with no lint errors. The five "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"require-await"},{"type":"text","value":" warnings in the leadership test predate this change."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The production diff is net "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"+62 lines"}]},{"type":"text","value":": a durable rejection marker, one shared cleanup boundary, a stopped-executor error latch, and a pre-admission check. Tests are net "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"+208 lines"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and contract documents net "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"+28 lines"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The latch does not schedule a recovery lifecycle. Automatic marked cleanup resumes only in a fresh executor after storage recovers."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"review-disposition","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Review disposition"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Item"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disposition at this executable revision"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R1: rejection-path delete error escaped before the handled-provider-error flag"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed by explicit storage-error propagation after recording the terminal marker. The batch stops; the affected waiter retains its original provider error."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R2: constant deletion retry delay and FIFO head blocking"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The constant-delay defect was real at the reviewed revision. The maintainer chose fail-stop, so no deletion retry or backoff remains. Queued peers stay untouched."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R3: failed terminal deletion could replay "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutationFn"},{"type":"text","value":" after restart"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed by durable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rejection-pending"},{"type":"text","value":". Both provider-call count and optimistic restoration are checked."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R4: repeated marker writes on deletion retry"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A marked row on restart now goes directly to removal. The original unconditional skip was unsafe after a failed first marker write; this revision stops on that failure."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R5: missing terminal-failure deletion dimension"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Added direct batch, public caller, FIFO timer, and same-storage restart witnesses to the settlement and leadership owners."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CodeRabbit CR5: backoff and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastError"},{"type":"text","value":" suggestion"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Overlaps R2. Backoff is superseded by fail-stop; overwriting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastError"},{"type":"text","value":" with the deletion error would erase the original provider failure needed for terminal replay."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"limits-and-open-work","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Limits and open work"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fake storage controls exact failure and restart order. Native browser restart, multi-owner handoff, physical power-loss durability, and real provider idempotency remain outside these witnesses. A failed phase write leaves an unmarked replay window; the provider must honor the supplied idempotency key. Already admitted queued callers remain pending when this executor stops. Their eventual disposition after a fresh executor starts is a separate lifecycle observation, owned by the settlement oracle and the broader offline policy in "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/1659","children":[{"type":"text","value":"RFC #1659"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The earlier review record remains an accurate description of its own revision and is not edited."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"offline-outbox-storage-failure-fail-stop-follow-up","text":"Offline outbox storage failure: fail-stop follow-up","level":1},{"id":"contract-and-evidence","text":"Contract and evidence","level":2},{"id":"review-disposition","text":"Review disposition","level":2},{"id":"limits-and-open-work","text":"Limits and open work","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Offline outbox storage failure: fail-stop follow-up\n\nExecutable revision: `d3d00a67fbc9509cde632578a9e36241caceb4e2`  \nPrior review: [Offline outbox deletion settlement](2026-09-30-offline-deletion-settlement.md).  \nReview inputs: the maintainer's four findings and test-gap note for PR #1968, plus CodeRabbit review 5373142655 at `400d8d86970b2cc6368b30a68554bbdcad734bfd`.\n\nThe maintainer decided that an outbox deletion failure means storage is seriously broken: stop the current executor and throw the storage error. This deliberately revises the earlier in-process deletion-retry law recorded at the prior executable revision. A phase-write failure takes the same fail-stop path because it prevents a durable record of the provider outcome.\n\n## Contract and evidence\n\n| Boundary | Executable witness at this revision |\n| --- | --- |\n| Successful provider, failed delete | `transaction-settlement.property.test.ts` holds a provider and queues a peer. After delete fails, `commit()` and `isPersisted.promise` reject with the exact storage error, the local transaction fails, the peer stays pending, and the head's durable `deletion-pending` marker remains. An online notification causes no second deletion or peer provider call. A later admission rejects before its outbox write. |\n| Permanent provider failure, failed delete | The direct executor witness rejects `execute()` with the exact storage error, rejects the transaction waiter with the original `NonRetriableError`, and retains both FIFO entries. Another `executeAll()` on the stopped executor rejects with the same storage error even after storage recovers. |\n| Failed phase write | The public witness rejects the head caller with the marker-write error, does not delete or retry, and leaves the queued peer untouched. The durable row is unmarked; a crash can still replay provider work. |\n| Restart after a durable marker | Separate successful and permanently rejected provider histories construct a fresh executor over the same storage. It removes the marked row without another provider call; the rejection path does not restore optimistic state. The successful path also checks that restart does not rewrite an already durable marker. |\n| Timer and leadership boundary | The leadership witness injects one failed terminal deletion, advances fake time by 60 seconds, and observes no automatic retry or later provider call. |\n\nThe two direct stop-and-throw witnesses were RED against the uncommitted retrying candidate: both `execute()` calls resolved when they had to reject. They are GREEN at this revision. The full offline suite passed **237/237 tests across 18 files**; package typecheck, build, Prettier, and edited-file lint passed with no lint errors. The five `require-await` warnings in the leadership test predate this change.\n\nThe production diff is net **+62 lines**: a durable rejection marker, one shared cleanup boundary, a stopped-executor error latch, and a pre-admission check. Tests are net **+208 lines** and contract documents net **+28 lines**. The latch does not schedule a recovery lifecycle. Automatic marked cleanup resumes only in a fresh executor after storage recovers.\n\n## Review disposition\n\n| Item | Disposition at this executable revision |\n| --- | --- |\n| R1: rejection-path delete error escaped before the handled-provider-error flag | Fixed by explicit storage-error propagation after recording the terminal marker. The batch stops; the affected waiter retains its original provider error. |\n| R2: constant deletion retry delay and FIFO head blocking | The constant-delay defect was real at the reviewed revision. The maintainer chose fail-stop, so no deletion retry or backoff remains. Queued peers stay untouched. |\n| R3: failed terminal deletion could replay `mutationFn` after restart | Fixed by durable `rejection-pending`. Both provider-call count and optimistic restoration are checked. |\n| R4: repeated marker writes on deletion retry | A marked row on restart now goes directly to removal. The original unconditional skip was unsafe after a failed first marker write; this revision stops on that failure. |\n| R5: missing terminal-failure deletion dimension | Added direct batch, public caller, FIFO timer, and same-storage restart witnesses to the settlement and leadership owners. |\n| CodeRabbit CR5: backoff and `lastError` suggestion | Overlaps R2. Backoff is superseded by fail-stop; overwriting `lastError` with the deletion error would erase the original provider failure needed for terminal replay. |\n\n## Limits and open work\n\nFake storage controls exact failure and restart order. Native browser restart, multi-owner handoff, physical power-loss durability, and real provider idempotency remain outside these witnesses. A failed phase write leaves an unmarked replay window; the provider must honor the supplied idempotency key. Already admitted queued callers remain pending when this executor stops. Their eventual disposition after a fresh executor starts is a separate lifecycle observation, owned by the settlement oracle and the broader offline policy in [RFC #1659](https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/1659). The earlier review record remains an accurate description of its own revision and is not edited.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Offline outbox storage failure: fail-stop follow-up\n\nExecutable revision: `d3d00a67fbc9509cde632578a9e36241caceb4e2`  \nPrior review: [Offline outbox deletion settlement](2026-09-30-offline-deletion-settlement.md).  \nReview inputs: the maintainer's four findings and test-gap note for PR #1968, plus CodeRabbit review 5373142655 at `400d8d86970b2cc6368b30a68554bbdcad734bfd`.\n\nThe maintainer decided that an outbox deletion failure means storage is seriously broken: stop the current executor and throw the storage error. This deliberately revises the earlier in-process deletion-retry law recorded at the prior executable revision. A phase-write failure takes the same fail-stop path because it prevents a durable record of the provider outcome.\n\n## Contract and evidence\n\n| Boundary | Executable witness at this revision |\n| --- | --- |\n| Successful provider, failed delete | `transaction-settlement.property.test.ts` holds a provider and queues a peer. After delete fails, `commit()` and `isPersisted.promise` reject with the exact storage error, the local transaction fails, the peer stays pending, and the head's durable `deletion-pending` marker remains. An online notification causes no second deletion or peer provider call. A later admission rejects before its outbox write. |\n| Permanent provider failure, failed delete | The direct executor witness rejects `execute()` with the exact storage error, rejects the transaction waiter with the original `NonRetriableError`, and retains both FIFO entries. Another `executeAll()` on the stopped executor rejects with the same storage error even after storage recovers. |\n| Failed phase write | The public witness rejects the head caller with the marker-write error, does not delete or retry, and leaves the queued peer untouched. The durable row is unmarked; a crash can still replay provider work. |\n| Restart after a durable marker | Separate successful and permanently rejected provider histories construct a fresh executor over the same storage. It removes the marked row without another provider call; the rejection path does not restore optimistic state. The successful path also checks that restart does not rewrite an already durable marker. |\n| Timer and leadership boundary | The leadership witness injects one failed terminal deletion, advances fake time by 60 seconds, and observes no automatic retry or later provider call. |\n\nThe two direct stop-and-throw witnesses were RED against the uncommitted retrying candidate: both `execute()` calls resolved when they had to reject. They are GREEN at this revision. The full offline suite passed **237/237 tests across 18 files**; package typecheck, build, Prettier, and edited-file lint passed with no lint errors. The five `require-await` warnings in the leadership test predate this change.\n\nThe production diff is net **+62 lines**: a durable rejection marker, one shared cleanup boundary, a stopped-executor error latch, and a pre-admission check. Tests are net **+208 lines** and contract documents net **+28 lines**. The latch does not schedule a recovery lifecycle. Automatic marked cleanup resumes only in a fresh executor after storage recovers.\n\n## Review disposition\n\n| Item | Disposition at this executable revision |\n| --- | --- |\n| R1: rejection-path delete error escaped before the handled-provider-error flag | Fixed by explicit storage-error propagation after recording the terminal marker. The batch stops; the affected waiter retains its original provider error. |\n| R2: constant deletion retry delay and FIFO head blocking | The constant-delay defect was real at the reviewed revision. The maintainer chose fail-stop, so no deletion retry or backoff remains. Queued peers stay untouched. |\n| R3: failed terminal deletion could replay `mutationFn` after restart | Fixed by durable `rejection-pending`. Both provider-call count and optimistic restoration are checked. |\n| R4: repeated marker writes on deletion retry | A marked row on restart now goes directly to removal. The original unconditional skip was unsafe after a failed first marker write; this revision stops on that failure. |\n| R5: missing terminal-failure deletion dimension | Added direct batch, public caller, FIFO timer, and same-storage restart witnesses to the settlement and leadership owners. |\n| CodeRabbit CR5: backoff and `lastError` suggestion | Overlaps R2. Backoff is superseded by fail-stop; overwriting `lastError` with the deletion error would erase the original provider failure needed for terminal replay. |\n\n## Limits and open work\n\nFake storage controls exact failure and restart order. Native browser restart, multi-owner handoff, physical power-loss durability, and real provider idempotency remain outside these witnesses. A failed phase write leaves an unmarked replay window; the provider must honor the supplied idempotency key. Already admitted queued callers remain pending when this executor stops. Their eventual disposition after a fresh executor starts is a separate lifecycle observation, owned by the settlement oracle and the broader offline policy in [RFC #1659](https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/1659). The earlier review record remains an accurate description of its own revision and is not edited.\n"}
